{
  "about": "Referrals: myReferral, applyReferralCode (REFERRAL_OFF, REFERRAL_USED, REFERRAL_TOO_LATE, REFERRAL_INVALID, REFERRAL_SELF, checked in that order; the welcome coupon it returns), the welcome coupon at checkout, and the referrer's reward when the referee's first qualifying order (subtotalPaise ≥ referral.minOrderPaise) is delivered through adminUpdateOrder: once, only for a pending referral, void when the referrer already has referral.monthlyCap rewards this IST month (users/{referrer}.referralRewards[\"yyyymm\"]). Ported from supabase/tests/offers.test.sql (Referrals) and scripts/api_e2e.py offers_checks, with the semantics of my_referral, apply_referral_code and reward_referral (20261030000000_offers.sql). The SQL walks an order through packed and shipped first; here each delivery case starts from a shipped order. This world holds no promotions, so \"promotions/*\" in \"then\" is the one the call created. Conventions beyond vector-format.md: a doc in \"given\" replaces the world doc of the same path whole (every given doc is written in full). A field expected as null matches null or absent. orders items[] are listed in variant-id order; match them by id. \"$orderId\" / \"$id\" = the id the call returned. A \"then\" key ending in \"/*\" means the one doc in that collection (the case starts with none there); \"<collection>/*\": null means the call creates none. Where an expected value was not asserted by the SQL test but re-derived from the SQL code (price_lines, evaluate_promotion, offer_open, reward_referral, apply_referral_code), the case \"about\" says \"derived\".",
